{"prdMarkdown":"# Product Requirements Document (PRD): User List Endpoint Pagination\n\n## 1. Problem Statement\nThe users list endpoint in `app.py` currently returns the entire user collection at once. As the dataset in the Ledger API grows, loading all user accounts in a single request degrades performance, increases memory and bandwidth consumption, and creates an unscalable experience for clients.\n\n## 2. Goals\n- Introduce page-number based pagination for the user listing endpoint in `app.py`.\n- Standardize pagination parameters with sensible defaults: default page size of 20 items and a maximum limit capped at 100 items.\n- Prevent system errors or unhandled exceptions when clients pass invalid or boundary pagination parameters.\n\n## 3. Non-Goals\n- Adding search, filtering, or custom multi-column sorting (sorting/filtering was skipped and is out of scope for this iteration).\n- Migrating to cursor-based or keyset pagination.\n- Modifying database schemas or tables in `models.py`.\n\n## 4. User Impact\n- **API Consumers & Frontend (`templates/index.html`)**: API clients will receive bounded data sets, improving page response times and reducing client-side payload parsing overhead. Depending on the final response format decision (envelope vs. flat array), existing consumers may experience a breaking contract change.\n- **End Users**: Snappier load times when viewing ledger accounts.\n\n## 5. Open Product Questions\n- **Response Contract**: Should the API payload be wrapped in a metadata envelope (e.g., `{ \"items\": [...], \"page\": 1, \"total\": 45 }`), or should pagination metadata be exposed via standard HTTP response headers (such as `Link` or custom headers) to avoid breaking flat list consumers?\n- **Out-of-Range Handling**: When a client requests a page number that exceeds total available pages, should the endpoint return an HTTP 404 Not Found error or an HTTP 200 OK with an empty array `[]`?\n- **UI Integration Scope**: Should `templates/index.html` (and associated styling in `static/style.css`) be updated to render pagination controls (e.g., Previous/Next page links, page number indicators) as part of this scope?","trdMarkdown":"# Technical Requirements Document (TRD): User List Endpoint Pagination\n\n## 1. Architecture Notes\n- **Framework & Libraries**: Leverages Flask 3.0.0 and Flask-SQLAlchemy 3.1.1 as specified in `requirements.txt`.\n- **Endpoint Handling (`app.py`)**:\n  - Update the user list handler in `app.py` to parse query parameters: `page` (default: 1) and `per_page` (default: 20, max limit: 100).\n  - Utilize Flask-SQLAlchemy's pagination utilities (e.g., `db.paginate` or `Select.paginate`) on the user model defined in `models.py`.\n  - Ensure queries enforce an explicit ordering (e.g., `order_by(User.id.asc())`) to prevent nondeterministic result ordering and page drifting.\n- **Frontend / Template (`templates/index.html`)**:\n  - Audit `templates/index.html` to ensure existing rendering logic does not break if the endpoint response structure changes from a list to a dictionary.\n\n## 2. Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **Invalid Query Parameters**: Query values for `page` or `per_page` that are non-integers, strings, floats, zero, or negative numbers must be handled cleanly (falling back to defaults or returning 400 Bad Request) rather than generating unhandled 500 exceptions in `app.py`.\n- **Upper Bound Enforcement**: Requests with `per_page > 100` must be capped to `100`.\n- **Empty / Exceeded Pages**: Flask-SQLAlchemy's `paginate` helper defaults to `error_out=True`, which raises a 404 when `page > total_pages`. Configuration must explicitly set `error_out=False` if returning an empty list is desired.\n- **Nondeterministic Ordering**: Without an explicit `ORDER BY`, SQL results across pages are not guaranteed to be stable, risking missing or duplicated records across consecutive pages.\n- **Breaking Contract Changes**: If switching to an envelope response payload, any consumer expecting a plain JSON list will fail.\n\n## 3. Engineering Questions\n- What specific database column and direction should be the deterministic default ordering key (e.g., user ID ascending or account creation timestamp descending) in `models.py`?\n- Should `per_page` values exceeding 100 be silently clamped to 100, or should the server reject them with a 400 Bad Request validation error?\n\n## 4. Estimate\n- **Expected effort**: 2-3 days.\n- **Worst case buffer**: Up to 7 days (accounting for potential UI updates in `templates/index.html` and contract migration considerations)."}